{"kind":"assert","text":"IR-1 report (audit only, at main d910468d).\n\n1. COST of counter-witness items 2 and 11 today. Each item's application scope is a lane scope with its own Durable Object and SQLite storage. Nothing deletes either one. Each scope object has one alarm, set to the earliest pending wake (packages/scope/src/outbox.ts:51-89, object.ts:226-229). An idle scope wakes only while a job is outstanding:\n- Archive. The admit filter excludes only register, membership and inbox, so these scopes archive (apps/room/archive.ts:48). After a confirmed pass, the stored wake is next: null, and a quiet scope never wakes (packages/scope/src/archive-row.ts:197).\n- Archive retry (AR-1). The floor is 60 s, and the backoff doubles to a cap of 3600 s (archive-driver.ts:90-95). At the cap, a failing archive costs 1 wake per hour per scope, about 24 per day. A wake makes a handful of DO requests. No end is tied to the installation state.\n- Outbox retry. Retries run every 1 s, growing to 300 s (packages/contract/src/bounds.ts:128-129), so an undelivered send costs at most 12 wakes per hour.\n- Deadlines, signer maintenance and credential expiry. These wake only when such a job exists.\nYour rate reads show dap-lending at 0 requests in every quiet minute, 12:22Z-12:42Z and 11:27Z-11:47Z. So both old scopes are idle now: their archives are confirmed and they have no outbox work. The present cost is storage only. The worst case at the AR-1 cap is 24 wakes per day per superseded item. The screen cost is nil, because no screen row names items 2 or 11.\n\n2. OPTIONS TODAY: none, as 4f1474e6 found. The installation item has three states, pending, active and refused (final) (packages/platform/src/application-install.ts:30-32). No act, CLI verb or role moves an item out of active. retire-definition (rules-scope.ts:281-292) retires a definition, not an installation, and existing scopes stay as they are.\n\nThe smallest act the rules could declare: add a final state 'retired' to the installation item, and an act retire-installation with step transition on installation, grant application.install (admin), guard state active and effect retired. It would touch four things:\n- (a) The rules scope. This is a new version of applicationInstallRulesScope, so each room needs a definition succession to use it.\n- (b) The installation reader. Today the screen route serves any trusted installation whose presentation matches (route.ts:75-79). It must refuse a retired item, or the route must check state.\n- (c) The archive. archive-proof.ts refuses any job that is not active with malformed-or-unsupported. A retired item would make that scope's archive fail on every pass and retry hourly forever, which is worse than today. The proof must accept retired, so that the final journal archives once and the wake then clears.\n- (d) The application scope. No scope-wide close exists (notes/2026-10-22-definition-succession.md:62-76), so its storage stays. Deleting storage would be a further act.\nThis is a kernel change: a new platform rules definition and an archive-proof rule. Hugh decides.\n\n3. RECOMMENDATION: no retire act now. Today a superseded item costs storage and, at worst, one hourly retry. A retire act needs a rules succession in every room and the archive change in (c), so it costs more than it saves. Instead, take to Hugh the replace form from decision 4 of notes/2026-10-15-self-contained-applications.md: an upgrade act that moves the item to a new release and makes the old scope final in one step, with (b) and (c) designed together. Until then, keep upgrades few, and read the DO rate after each install.","body":{"to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}